{
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "score": 7,
  "estimated_minutes": 70,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish OHIC’s primary and umbrella capacities, understand that ERC reinsured only the excess coverage, and identify the coverage questions reserved for Phase 1 summary judgment."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding the disputed interest requires Wisconsin’s settlement-offer interest and direct-action rules, together with the insurance distinction between covered loss and supplementary claim expenses."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "The constructions link the Primary Policy’s prejudgment-interest endorsement, the Umbrella Policy’s exclusions, and Reinsurance Agreement Articles I, III, IV, V, and IX."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The court states its conclusions clearly, but the Article V construction requires following cross-references, the Holland comparison, and the self-insured-retention explanation rather than reading the indemnity clause alone."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "The claim-expense definition dispute in d03 and Article V limitation in d04 share the same reasoning, while d05 and d06 test that construction through proportionality and participation provisions."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d04 — Understanding the limitation 'as respects reinsurance afforded by the other terms of this agreement' requires tracing Articles I, III, IV, and V and distinguishing eligibility for expense reimbursement from the formula determining its amount. The reviewer must also separate this construction from the closely overlapping definition dispute in d03 and the supporting arguments in d05 and d06.",
  "reason": "Assign this case to a reviewer comfortable tracing layered insurance coverage across instruments, rather than treating each disputed phrase independently. The principal challenge is keeping OHIC’s two insurer capacities separate while following how a payment becomes primary-policy expense, umbrella-policy loss, or reinsured loss. The opinion supplies the necessary statutory background and generally makes its holdings explicit, so extensive outside research should not be necessary. A coverage map and a focused reread of the linked reinsurance provisions should produce confident understanding, but the overlapping claim-expense constructions make this more demanding than an ordinary clause-interpretation case."
}
